The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT) && (in_nmi() || in_hardirq()))
    return NULL;
```
```c
_raw_spin_trylock(&n->list_lock); // always succeeds on UP
```
Reason: On UP kernels, spin_trylock() is a no‑op that unconditionally succeeds. The existing guard only handles PREEMPT_RT; when kmalloc_nolock_noprof() is called from NMI context on UP, it proceeds to call into the slab allocator (e.g., get_from_partial_node()) which uses _raw_spin_trylock() to acquire n->list_lock. Since the trylock never fails, it can re‑enter and corrupt slab state already locked by the interrupted context.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* On UP, spin_trylock() always succeeds even when it is locked */
if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SMP) && in_nmi())
    return NULL;
```
Reason: This early return directly prevents the re‑entry into the slab allocator from NMI context on UP kernels. By returning NULL before any spin_trylock is attempted, the corrupting acquisition of an already held lock is avoided.

